1
00:00:00,350 --> 00:00:03,550
Production settings.

2
00:00:04,350 --> 00:00:08,225
Here is what wagtail start gave us for production.

3
00:00:08,225 --> 00:00:10,627
Debug off, one storage setting,

4
00:00:10,627 --> 00:00:15,199
and an import of a local settings file that does not exist.

5
00:00:15,199 --> 00:00:18,221
Django's deploy check finds six issues.

6
00:00:18,221 --> 00:00:20,856
And there is no secret key at all.

7
00:00:21,656 --> 00:00:24,949
And before any of that, the biggest one.

8
00:00:24,949 --> 00:00:31,122
The Dockerfile that wagtail start generates runs gunicorn on the WSGI file,

9
00:00:31,122 --> 00:00:34,168
and never says which settings to use.

10
00:00:34,168 --> 00:00:37,954
So I loaded it exactly the way gunicorn would.

11
00:00:37,954 --> 00:00:39,683
Development settings.

12
00:00:39,683 --> 00:00:40,423
Debug on.

13
00:00:40,423 --> 00:00:41,164
Any host.

14
00:00:41,164 --> 00:00:44,786
And the secret key that is committed to git.

15
00:00:45,586 --> 00:00:51,146
Deployed as generated, any error on the live site shows a full traceback,

16
00:00:51,146 --> 00:00:53,355
settings included, to anyone.

17
00:00:53,355 --> 00:00:59,676
So the WSGI file now defaults to production, and the Dockerfile says so explicitly.

18
00:00:59,676 --> 00:01:03,408
And production refuses to start without a secret.

19
00:01:03,408 --> 00:01:08,740
If something is missing, the server stops, with a message saying what.

20
00:01:08,740 --> 00:01:10,492
That is failing closed.

21
00:01:10,492 --> 00:01:16,204
manage.py still defaults to development, so nothing changes on your laptop.

22
00:01:17,004 --> 00:01:22,348
Everything secret or host specific now comes from environment variables,

23
00:01:22,348 --> 00:01:27,247
so nothing sensitive is in git and the same code runs on any host.

24
00:01:27,247 --> 00:01:28,509
Two are required.

25
00:01:28,509 --> 00:01:31,329
The secret key, and the allowed hosts.

26
00:01:31,329 --> 00:01:37,119
An empty hosts list would start perfectly happily and then answer every single

27
00:01:37,119 --> 00:01:40,830
request with a 400, so we refuse to start instead.

28
00:01:41,630 --> 00:01:43,826
The database is one variable.

29
00:01:43,826 --> 00:01:44,811
Database URL.

30
00:01:44,811 --> 00:01:50,264
Set it to a Postgres address and it parses to Django's Postgres backend.

31
00:01:50,264 --> 00:01:53,975
Leave it unset and you get the local SQLite file,

32
00:01:53,975 --> 00:01:59,958
which means you can try these production settings on a laptop before any server

33
00:01:59,958 --> 00:02:00,488
exists.

34
00:02:01,288 --> 00:02:06,017
Point it at Postgres, and it fails before it even tries to connect.

35
00:02:06,017 --> 00:02:10,816
On Postgres, Wagtail's search switches to Postgres full text search,

36
00:02:10,816 --> 00:02:14,133
and that needs Django's Postgres app installed.

37
00:02:14,133 --> 00:02:16,815
So add it when the engine is Postgres.

38
00:02:16,815 --> 00:02:19,850
And while testing with no server listening,

39
00:02:19,850 --> 00:02:23,449
a wrong address took more than two minutes to fail.

40
00:02:23,449 --> 00:02:28,954
A ten second connect timeout, and the same mistake shows up in twelve seconds.

41
00:02:29,754 --> 00:02:36,966
Now run the site with production settings, before collecting static files.

42
00:02:36,966 --> 00:02:38,818
The home page, 500.

43
00:02:38,818 --> 00:02:40,864
The admin login, 500.

44
00:02:40,864 --> 00:02:43,496
Even the 404 page is a 500.

45
00:02:43,496 --> 00:02:47,004
Missing static files manifest entry.

46
00:02:47,804 --> 00:02:53,961
The manifest storage will not render a static tag for any file it has not seen.

47
00:02:53,961 --> 00:02:58,404
So without collect static, every page on the site breaks,

48
00:02:58,404 --> 00:03:02,690
including the admin login you would use to investigate.

49
00:03:02,690 --> 00:03:03,860
Collect static.

50
00:03:03,860 --> 00:03:06,354
Two hundred and seventeen files.

51
00:03:06,354 --> 00:03:11,030
And now everything is 200, and a missing page is a real 404.

52
00:03:11,830 --> 00:03:16,167
Which means, back in episode four, I promised this.

53
00:03:16,167 --> 00:03:23,056
The 404 template that extends our base, finally showing up, because debug is off.

54
00:03:23,856 --> 00:03:27,555
With debug off, Django stops serving static files,

55
00:03:27,555 --> 00:03:30,514
so without help the stylesheet is a 404.

56
00:03:30,514 --> 00:03:34,509
White noise serves it from the app itself, compressed.

57
00:03:34,509 --> 00:03:36,432
And look at the file name.

58
00:03:36,432 --> 00:03:41,611
It has a hash in it, so the browser is told to cache it for ten years.

59
00:03:41,611 --> 00:03:44,274
Change the CSS and the hash changes.

60
00:03:44,274 --> 00:03:50,192
That is the permanent fix for the cache trap that cost us ten minutes in episode

61
00:03:50,192 --> 00:03:50,562
four.

62
00:03:51,362 --> 00:03:53,022
But look at the hero.

63
00:03:53,022 --> 00:03:53,575
Broken.

64
00:03:53,575 --> 00:04:00,215
Uploaded images are media, not static files, and with debug off nothing serves them.

65
00:04:00,215 --> 00:04:02,903
White noise deliberately does not.

66
00:04:02,903 --> 00:04:08,120
You can see the alt text from episode six doing its job, at least.

67
00:04:08,120 --> 00:04:14,444
How media is served depends on the host, so that is decided in the next episode.

68
00:04:15,244 --> 00:04:16,077
And HTTPS.

69
00:04:16,077 --> 00:04:22,490
The host terminates the encryption and forwards plain requests with a header,

70
00:04:22,490 --> 00:04:24,988
so Django is told to trust it.

71
00:04:24,988 --> 00:04:27,071
Plain HTTP now redirects.

72
00:04:27,071 --> 00:04:31,235
A request for somebody else's hostname is refused.

73
00:04:31,235 --> 00:04:33,234
Cookies are secure only.

74
00:04:33,234 --> 00:04:35,566
And HSTS starts at one hour.

75
00:04:35,566 --> 00:04:38,064
It is a promise browsers keep.

76
00:04:38,064 --> 00:04:41,396
Set a year, then break your certificate,

77
00:04:41,396 --> 00:04:45,643
and nobody can reach the site until the year is up.

78
00:04:45,643 --> 00:04:48,142
Raise it once HTTPS is proven.

79
00:04:48,942 --> 00:04:50,688
Django's deploy check.

80
00:04:50,688 --> 00:04:56,880
Six issues down to two, and both of those are about committing harder to HSTS,

81
00:04:56,880 --> 00:05:01,008
which we are not doing until the real domain exists.

82
00:05:01,808 --> 00:05:04,495
And one more from the generated files.

83
00:05:04,495 --> 00:05:10,009
The requirements file that the Dockerfile installs still caps Django below six

84
00:05:10,009 --> 00:05:10,716
point one.

85
00:05:10,716 --> 00:05:14,180
Every episode has run on six point one point one.

86
00:05:14,180 --> 00:05:19,695
So a production image would have been a different Django from anything we ever

87
00:05:19,695 --> 00:05:20,190
tested.

88
00:05:20,190 --> 00:05:24,290
Both requirements files now pin exactly the same versions.

89
00:05:25,090 --> 00:05:27,089
And to be straight about it.

90
00:05:27,089 --> 00:05:29,445
The Postgres address, the driver,

91
00:05:29,445 --> 00:05:33,086
the system checks and the timeout are all verified.

92
00:05:33,086 --> 00:05:35,513
A live Postgres connection is not,

93
00:05:35,513 --> 00:05:39,226
because there is no database server on this machine,

94
00:05:39,226 --> 00:05:42,153
and neither is building the Docker image.

95
00:05:42,153 --> 00:05:45,580
Both happen against the real host, next episode.

96
00:05:46,380 --> 00:05:48,894
Commit, and tag.

97
00:05:49,694 --> 00:05:52,140
Next episode, the last one.

98
00:05:52,140 --> 00:05:58,664
A real host, a real database, media that works, and the first live edit.

99
00:05:59,464 --> 00:06:04,749
Next episode, we deploy it.

100
00:06:04,749 --> 00:06:08,664
Thanks for watching.
